well the debate on the second referendum  is raging in the Labor Party and today  the people's bake campaign have released  a survey of 25,000 people suggesting  labour could fall sixteen points behind  the Conservatives if they allow brexit  to go through well joining us now is the  Labour MP Chaka Munna thank you very  much for being with us happy new year  well I guess we're hoping first of new  year fresh start try and get some  movement when it comes to the gridlock  that we've seem to be in your answer to  all this is a second referendum  how many MPs do you think are now in  support of having another vote I think  first of all we've actually got to have  the meaningful vote on the prime  minister so-called deal I'm not going to  be disingenuous and pretend that we have  the numbers for a people's vote on the  terms of our withdrawal from the  European Union right now but obviously  what happens and now and happy before  now will be very different after we've  had that vote let's be absolutely clear  the prime minister gave the pledge on  the 17th of December at the despatch-box  in the House of Commons that we would  have that meaningful vote in the week of  the 14th of January now she doesn't hold  that vote  she arguably will have misled the House  of Commons and there will be moves on a  cross-party basis from the back benches  to ensure that the will of the house is  tested on whether or not they wish to  support her so-called deal so that's it  what would happen if the government yeah  there are lays the vote again there have  been reports that's right there are  various parliamentary tools that we can  use to illustrate the will of the house  and frankly if the government doesn't  comply with the terms of the EU  withdrawal at 2018 which provides for  government to do a statement on the 21st  of January and then there's a  determination as a motion will forward  as to what will happen next then  arguably the government would be in  contempt of Parliament and so they  should be in no doubt that if we have  further delay which would be grossly  irresponsible  we've got 82 days to go to a brexit exit  day just over 11 weeks and one thing  that people who may not necessarily be  convinced of a people's vote are  convinced of is of the need to avoid a  No Deal brexit none of our constituents  I think voted for that  and they certainly didn't vote to make  themselves poorer either I mean I feel  that none of you very small number  perhaps of European citizens would like  to see that year and one of the most  remain supporting constituencies in the  country that's right in other parts of  the country polling shows that actually  there is a significant number of people  who would prefer a No Deal for example  the Prime Minister's do well I don't  think we can say categorically one way  or another what the will of the people  is actually in 2080 you're right you're  right to pick me up right to pick me up  on that but frankly that is the whole  reason why we've been arguing for the  last few months for a people's vote to  determine what it is now because 2016  was one thing but we don't really any of  us can none of us can really say for  certain what the will of the people is  in 2018 not least you know there are  over 2 million young people who can now  vote who couldn't vote before who polls  would indicate overwhelmingly do not  want to see the country going down this  trajectory and I think look for the  Labour Party let's not forget that in  2017 a huge number of younger  generations voted for the Labour Party  hoping that we would take the country on  a different trajectory and I'm not sure  we will be forgiven for robbing them of  their futures if we don't actually give  them a say on this the because you see  no blame labour for that  I think they'll blame it with a plague  on all your houses but given that you  know we know that younger people  overwhelmingly have been supporting the  Labour Party of late it's quite clear  that they're not going to look well on  the labour party that they see  threatening their future prospects we've  all got to bear we've all got to bear  that in mind in my view one thing that I  am struggle to get my head around is  that the people's vote campaign is  trying to push through a referendum that  many people will see as divisive many  people will see it as a betrayal of the  first referendum and yet you can't even  agree on the details of what that would  be what do you think should be on the  ballot paper because it seems that  whenever I ask people I get a different  answer well ultimately this is something  for Parliament to determine and let's  not forget though if you a campaign well  hang on just a minute how in just a  minute if you look at the people's vote  campaign we have people from the left to  the right of politics we've got  nationalists from the SNP from plied we  have people who have a different view on  things all together in this big  10 so from a point of view of unity of  campaign I don't think asides us for not  having the collected group of people I'm  quite clear that clearly the current  deal that we have as a member of the  European Union the government's own  research shows this is the best deal  that we could possibly have so that has  to be on the ballot paper and I do think  you have to have a option on that ballot  paper that would please the likes of  Peter bow that is a hard brexit so to  speak those two choices is in some way  shape or form would need to be presented  to the British people and of course what  you know the position we're in now is  very different the argument we were  having before two years ago was based on  hypotheticals of what could happen if we  voted to leave the European Union now we  actually know what will happen we have  much more of a concrete picture and we  know that you know however you vote  either in three options then well I  would say two or three I'm pretty  open-minded about it but ultimately it's  for Parliament and of course the  Electoral Commission to determine but by  the way let's just be out you know let's  not presume that the Prime Minister's  deal isn't going to pass many people  have been focusing you think it might  past who knows because we don't know  what she's going to announce she's going  to be going on another program in an  hour's time we don't know what she's  going to say there we don't know what  she's going to be here we don't know if  she's going to be say in Parliament this  week and I don't think we can be  complacent the problem with this deal is  not only the backstop which people have  a concern threatens a territorial  integrity of the United Kingdom it is  that for the first time certainly in our  lives we have a government pursuing a  course which it has already told us is  going to make our country poorer that is  an extraordinary thing this deal that  she's putting forward to people is going  to do a measurable damage to our economy  I mean there's a certain irony in the  man on Sunday today she is saying that  if MPs don't vote for a deal it's going  to threaten jobs she's already put out  research her own economic impact  assessments showing that her deal is  going to threaten people's jobs and  businesses in this country I want to  talk to you about no deal because it's  nearly something that people on your  side of the argument are very concerned  about can Parliament actually stop no  deal happening because surely it's the  default position if you can't agree well  it's a very good question  legally there are two things that would  need to happen  first of all in order to change exit day  which is stipulated in law to be the  29th of March of this year  Minister will have to put forward a  regulation to change that date now we're  unclear as to whether that necessitates  a vote of the House of Commons but it  would have to be at the initiative of a  minister secondly we would have to get  an extension to the article 50 process  from the European Union and this is the  key thing for me  I accept that there are some colleagues  both on the Tory and the Labour benches  across the house who have misgivings  about having a people's vote but there  is one thing the European Union have  been absolutely clear about if you want  an extension to article 50 we are not  going to grant you an extension to give  you more time for renegotiation as has  been mooted by some that is not going to  happen but we will grant you an  extension for a democratic vote so the  people's vote if in the short - the  medium term you want to avoid No Deal  that is your route to stopping our  country crashing out of the European  Union in a very disastrous way another  important point I'm Sophie it has been  mooted in the papers over the last few  days but if the Prime Minister fails to  get her plan through the House of  Commons she will simply keep bringing it  back again and again to the House of  Commons I've consulted with the class of  the House of Commons on this you cannot  simply bring the same motion again and  again and again so according to the  Clarkes she cannot bring it you cannot  do that and even if say you sought to  bring a different motion through  changing one word if it's in substance  it's the same thing under the rules of  the House of Commons  you can't just breathe bringing it again  and again and again and what I would say  to the Prime Minister is listen to the  House of Commons she turns up every time  gives us his e-statements tells us her  deal is the best deal Parliament says no  including on her own benches and she  tries to ignore it you cannot carry on  putting your head in the sand when we  have the clock basically is still  ticking and that she should be a no  doubt certainly in terms of what the  back benches are saying where so many of  the initiatives for on brexit seem to  have been coming over the last few  months we will not stand for a No Deal  scenario and we're not going to allow  her to simply delay this vote further  there are different parliamentary tools  at our disposal and we will use them and  just finally if you heard of CNP no  according to Westminster gossips and  Evening Standard its chukkas new park  well they're gonna happen well let's say  two things about let me tell you two  things about that  these two think this thing this concept  arises in two scenarios one when the  people's vote campaign is going  full-throttle and making a success and  brexit ear voices frankly at the Labor  Party and the Conservative Party peddled  this nonsense the second thing is when  the ugly face of anti-semitism rears its  head in the Labor Party - and what this  is used is a huge deflection exercise  from the real issues here first of all  the Glade party has to deal with  anti-semitism as I've said to you before  on this show and secondly let's stop  messing around let's focus on the real  issue here who is it gonna happen then I  don't think oh look I want us to stop  this disaster that's happening  everything else is deflection everything  else is distraction and I don't get to  determine these things as I said you  know in terms of any new parties all  that it's not for me to determine as  I've said to you on this show before  that's for the leadership of the Labour  Party and frankly the Conservative Party  - they want to keep their parties  together that's for them not for me okay  thank you very much

